# mounts — the ONE mount table for this stack: what the hub has, and what the boxes have.
#
# Project set-up, not runtime state: root-owned, synced by Ansible, applied when a container is
# CREATED. There is no command that edits it from inside the hub — change it here, then
#   ./gen-hub-mounts.sh && docker compose up -d hub && cbx recreate all
#
# Both sides in ONE file on purpose. The hub and the boxes must agree path-for-path (installed
# dependencies bake absolute paths in), and when the two lists were maintained separately they drifted:
# a `~/.gradle` shared rw with the hub let its long-running `bootRun` hold gradle's cache locks against
# every box indefinitely. One row per path, a mode per side, no way to change one and forget the other.
# The broker reads the BOX column; gen-hub-mounts.sh renders the HUB column into compose.override.yml,
# and the hub's entrypoint warns at boot if what it actually has doesn't match this file.
#
# Grammar (whitespace-separated; '#'/blank ignored):
#
#   CHECKOUT <dst> <rw|ro>          The box's working copy at /home/dev/<dst> (default: 'repo rw'):
#                                   an overlayfs view of the current golden (data/golden/current) with
#                                   the box's own upper layer, which is what makes N agents cost
#                                   N x (their own diff) instead of N full checkouts, each with a real
#                                   .git on its own branch (agent/<box>). `ro` binds the golden itself
#                                   instead — no upper layer, no writes, no git bootstrap (a
#                                   look-but-don't-touch box). Box-side by definition: the hub has the
#                                   real repo, not a golden.
#
#   <src> <dst> <hub> <box>         One path, mounted at /home/dev/<dst> on either side.
#
#     src   ./x   relative to the stack dir (this directory)
#           /x    an absolute host path outside the stack
#           x     relative to the golden, and confined to it — box-side only (hub mode must be '-')
#
#     mode  rw       plain bind mount, writable
#           ro       plain bind mount, read-only
#           overlay  <src> as a shared read-only LOWER layer with a per-box upper layer on top:
#                    shared content, private writes. BOX SIDE ONLY. The upper layer lives in
#                    data/boxes/<box>/ovl-<key>/ and survives kill/recreate.
#                    ONLY FOR A SOURCE NOBODY WRITES TO while boxes are up: the kernel requires an
#                    immutable lowerdir, and a golden (sealed at snapshot time) is the case that
#                    qualifies. For a LIVE cache the hub keeps writing to, use cow-keep — see the
#                    gradle note below.
#           cow      a PRIVATE, WRITABLE copy of <src> for this box, made with
#                    `cp -a --reflink=always` (btrfs/xfs: shared extents, so near-instant and near-
#                    zero disk until something is written). BOX SIDE ONLY. Lives in
#                    data/boxes/<box>/cow-<key>/, is re-copied from <src> on every spawn/recreate,
#                    and is DELETED when the box is killed. Scratch space that starts from the hub's
#                    current state.
#           cow-keep the same private copy, KEPT across kill/recreate as the box's warm cache (like
#                    an overlay upper layer). It is re-copied from <src> only on a --fresh spawn.
#                    BOX SIDE ONLY. This is the right mode for a toolchain cache.
#           -        not mounted on this side
#
#   Both cow modes are one-way: nothing a box writes ever flows back to the hub, and a long-lived
#   cow-keep box drifts from the hub's cache until it is refreshed with --fresh. That was equally
#   true of `overlay`'s upper layer. Do NOT "fix" it by making the row rw — that is the
#   cross-container lock deadlock below, back again.
#   `--reflink=always`, never `auto`: if <src> and data/boxes are not on the same reflink-capable
#   filesystem the spawn FAILS loudly instead of quietly byte-copying multi-GB trees once per box.
#
# Default (this file absent or empty): just the overlay checkout at /home/dev/repo.

# <src>                              <dst>            <hub>  <box>
CHECKOUT                             repo             rw

# npm cache: genuinely shared rw. npm's cacache is content-addressed and safe for concurrent writers.
./data/npm-cache                     .npm             rw     rw

# Gradle home: a private reflinked COPY per box (cow-keep), never rw and never overlay.
#   never rw:      gradle holds its cache locks for the whole build — and `bootRun` never finishes —
#                  handing them over only when the waiting process pings the holder on LOCALHOST.
#                  Between containers that ping cannot arrive, so a shared rw ~/.gradle made one long
#                  build block every box's gradle forever ("Owner PID: <a pid you can't see>",
#                  waiting on caches/journal-1).
#   never overlay: the lower layer would be the hub's LIVE cache, and overlayfs requires an immutable
#                  lowerdir. Gradle's immutable workspaces (caches/<ver>/dependencies-accessors/<hash>)
#                  are rm -rf'd and renamed over on nearly every hub build; once that happens under a
#                  live overlay the box can list the directory but rm/mv/rmdir fail with EIO/ENOENT
#                  ("Directory not empty"), and every build then burns seconds in
#                  AssignImmutableWorkspaceStep "Could not move inconsistent immutable workspace".
# cow-keep gives each box its own writable copy — free on btrfs (reflink shares the extents), warm
# across kill/recreate, re-seeded from the hub only on a --fresh spawn.
./data/gradle-cache                  .gradle          rw     cow-keep

# Jenkins' pre-populated Maven repository, read-only on both sides so no build can ever mutate it.
# This is the one source OUTSIDE the stack dir: the broker cannot see it, docker resolves it on the
# host, and a wrong path is silently bound as an EMPTY DIR — check it first if deps stop resolving.
/var/jenkins_home/.m2/repository     .m2/repository   ro     ro

# Examples:

# Expose a subdirectory of the golden read-only at a second path (reference material for the agents)
#   docs  reference  -  ro

# A read-only box that can look at the code but never change it
#   CHECKOUT  repo  ro

# Scratch that starts from the hub's current state and dies with the box (re-copied on every spawn,
# removed by `cbx kill`)
#   ./data/testdata  testdata  rw  cow
